The bell above the door chimed a tired note as Elias slipped the brass key into the lock of his workshop. Inside, the air smelled of oil and old paper, and rows of gears lay like sleeping insects on velvet trays. He lifted a slender wooden case, its surface etched with spirals that seemed to pulse in the dim light. With deliberate hands he placed a tiny silver pendulum inside, then sealed the glass front with a sigh. When the first tick sounded, it lagged a heartbeat behind the world outside, as if the clock itself were breathing a fraction slower. Outside, the town marched on, unaware that time in this room was already learning a new rhythm.

